<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> What does it mean to say that "architecture begins where function ends"?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">Architecture must have no function.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">Architecture is any building that has a roof.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">A building becomes architecture when it does more than shelter, using space, light, and proportion to produce a specific experience.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Only decorations count as architecture.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A warehouse shelters goods; a cathedral shelters a congregation and also produces the sense of being in the presence of something larger than yourself.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Both a warehouse and a cathedral are buildings. The difference is what the cathedral's space, light, and proportion do beyond shelter.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> The four requirements of architecture are</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">technical, financial, political, and decorative.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">technical, functional, spatial, and revelatory.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">spatial, structural, musical, and visual.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">practical, historical, religious, and legal.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A building must stand, serve its purpose, organize space meaningfully, and reveal the values of the people who built and use it.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The four are technical (it stands), functional (it works), spatial (it organizes space), and revelatory (it reveals values).</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> What does the revelatory requirement ask of a building?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">That it never change.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">That it be as tall as possible.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">That it be built from a single material.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">That it say something about the values of the people who built it and use it.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The revelatory requirement is what raises a building from construction to art: it shows what a culture cares about.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Revelatory means revealing. The question is what the building reveals about the values of its builders and users.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> Why does a strip mall usually fail to qualify as architecture in this sense?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">It stands and functions, but it does not organize space meaningfully or reveal values clearly.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">It is too small.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">It is not technically sound.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">It is always built from the wrong materials.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Most buildings satisfy the first two requirements easily. The harder two, spatial and revelatory, are what distinguish architecture.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">A strip mall meets the first two requirements (technical and functional). Ask which two it does not meet.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> Which qualities does earth-rooted architecture emphasize?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">Height, light, and weightlessness.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">Connection to the ground, the site, gravity, raw materials, and centrality.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">Movement and flow without a site.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">Transparency and elevation on columns.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Wright's Fallingwater is the classic example: built over a waterfall from local stone, inseparable from its setting.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Earth-rooted architecture grows from its site. Fallingwater uses local stone and sits over a waterfall.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> Which features does sky-oriented architecture use to make stone appear to soar?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">Wooden beams only.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">Pointed arches, ribbed vaults, and flying buttresses.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">Thick walls and tiny windows.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">Flat roofs and low ceilings.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Gothic cathedrals like Chartres use these structural devices to reach upward and let in light.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Think of a Gothic cathedral. Its pointed arches, ribbed vaults, and flying buttresses all work to lift the stone and open it to light.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> What is the Farnsworth House an example of?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">Architecture that fails the technical requirement.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">Sky-oriented architecture.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">Earth-resting architecture: a glass box elevated on steel columns that sits lightly on the ground.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">Earth-rooted architecture.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. It neither roots into the ground nor reaches away from it. It rests lightly on it.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The lesson names a third category for a house that sits lightly on its site without rooting into it or reaching upward.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> How does Le Corbusier's Ronchamp chapel use light?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">Thick walls with irregular windows let light in as small, intense points, producing an intimate, contemplative interior.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">It uses no natural light at all.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">A central oculus is its only light source.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">Broad walls of glass flood the room with even light.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. This is the opposite of a Gothic cathedral's soaring transparency, though both are sky-oriented and use light as a primary element.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Remember the cave-like interior. Ronchamp's thick walls pierced by irregular windows send light in as separate bright points.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> Why does Zaha Hadid's Heydar Aliyev Center test the earth-rooted and sky-oriented categories?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">It is a perfect example of Gothic architecture.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">It is hidden underground.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">It has no roof.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">It does neither clearly: its surface folds continuously, embracing the ground and then lifting away in one gesture.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The building demonstrates that the lesson's framework is a starting point, not a cage.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The center has no clear anchor to its site and does not reach upward like a cathedral. Look for the answer about a continuous, flowing form.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> The Pantheon in Rome admits daylight only through a circular opening at the top of its dome. What is that opening called?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">The oculus.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">The apse.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">The buttress.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">The nave.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The oculus lets a disc of sunlight slowly cross the interior through the day, so the space makes you feel enclosed by the sky.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The Pantheon's round opening at the top of the dome has a Latin name meaning eye: the oculus.</div>
  </div>
</div>

